# P1 / D9. Deny-by-default: exclude everything, then re-admit exactly what the
# installed extension needs. The alternative — listing what to exclude — is how
# `.vscode-test/` (a ~280 MB VS Code download) ends up inside a `.vsix`, and
# nothing warns you.
#
# `scripts/check-vsix-contents.mjs` asserts the resulting file list, so this
# file being read the way it is meant to be is checked rather than assumed.
**

# The bundle esbuild produces, plus the real files it ships beside it: the
# venue templates and the Writing Skills are materialized to disk by the
# scaffolder and the skill loader, so they cannot be inlined (ADR 0010, 0028).
!dist/extension.cjs
!dist/webview.js
!dist/webview.css
!dist/venues/**
!dist/skills/**

# PDFium's WASM (V3, ADR 0080 section 9). A copied asset, like the two above:
# the extension reads it back at runtime through context.extensionUri, so it
# has to be a real file in the package. About 2 MiB deflated.
!dist/pdfium.wasm

# Source maps are excluded deliberately: they roughly double the package and an
# installed extension has no use for them.

# The brand marks the manifest names, and the listing artwork.
!media/**

# The manifest and its two localization bundles.
!package.json
!package.nls.json
!package.nls.zh-cn.json

# The listing page, the changelog tab, the licence the manifest points at, and
# the third-party attributions — all four travel with the distribution.
!README.md
!CHANGELOG.md
!LICENSE.txt
!THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md
